---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const iepCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Identification', note: 'A learner struggles; the school screens, evaluates (with parental consent) and determines eligibility under one of the recognised disability categories.' },
    { math: '2 · The IEP meeting', note: 'Parents sit as equal team members. Present levels, annual goals, services, accommodations and placement are written — with the parent\'s signature.' },
    { math: '3 · Goals are measurable', note: '"Will read better" is not a goal. "Will read 60 words per minute on grade-level text by May, measured weekly" is. Measurability is the law\'s demand.' },
    { math: '4 · Least restrictive environment', note: 'The default is the general classroom with supports; removal is justified only when education there cannot be achieved satisfactorily.' },
    { math: '5 · Progress and review', note: 'Goals are tracked with data, reported to families, and the IEP is revised at least annually. The document is a promise with a review date.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/special-education/foundational/special-education-law-and-the-iep">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Special education is teaching reshaped by law: a system in which a learner's disability
    creates enforceable rights to evaluation, individualised planning and education in the least
    restrictive setting. Its centrepiece is the IEP — the Individualised Education Program — a
    legal document that is also, at its best, the best teaching plan in the school.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from identification to reviewed promise: the IEP cycle" widget="equationStep" config={iepCfg} />

  <h2>IDEA changed the default</h2>
  <p>
    Before the 1975 US law (then EHA, now IDEA), schools could legally exclude children with
    disabilities. IDEA inverted the default: every eligible child is entitled to a free
    appropriate public education, parents are decision-making members of the team, and removal
    from the mainstream must be justified. Similar guarantees now run across jurisdictions — the
    professional standards (CEC's) teach the rights architecture as core knowledge.
  </p>

  <h2>Measurable goals are the system's engine</h2>
  <p>
    The law's demand for measurable goals is also good pedagogy: weekly progress data turns
    teaching into a feedback loop. When a goal's graph stays flat, the intervention changes —
    not the expectation. This data-based individualisation (a later unit's full subject) is what
    separates special education from well-intentioned accommodation.
  </p>

  <h2>Least restrictive environment, honestly applied</h2>
  <p>
    Inclusion is the starting assumption, not the reward. The continuum exists — consultation,
    co-teaching, resource rooms, special classes — but each step away from the general classroom
    requires the team to document why a lesser restriction cannot work. The craft of the
    profession is making that documentation reflect genuine planning rather than convenience.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> CEC Initial Preparation Standards; IDEA 2004 regulations; Friend &amp; Bursuck, <em>Including Students with Special Needs</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
